Shadowed interface declarations have distinct identities
effect Reader { Old : () => Int; } sig first : [Reader] (() -> Int) fun first() { do Old } effect Reader { New : () => Int; } (first : [Reader] (() -> Int))
stderr : @.*Type error: The modalities require different effect contexts.*
exit : 1

Interface names in an error use the scope of the failing binding
module Library { effect Reader { Old : () => Int; } effect Other { Pick : () => Int; } sig first : [Reader] (() -> Int) fun first() { do Old } sig second : [Other] (() -> Int) fun second() { do Pick } var mixed = if (true) first else second; effect Reader { New : () => Int; } } ()
stderr : @.*Type error: Expected \[Reader\] \(\(\) -> Int\), got \[Other\] \(\(\) -> Int\).*
exit : 1

An interface arity error names the declaration being used
module Library { module Nested { effect Reader(a::Abs) { Ask : () => a; } } } open Library; module Library { module Nested { effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } } } typename T = [Nested.Reader] Int;
stderr : @.*Arity mismatch: Type Nested\.Reader expects 1 type arguments, but 0 arguments were provided.*
exit : 1

Shadowing an interface still rejects duplicate operations in its scope
effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } ()
stderr : @.*Module Error: Duplicate operation Ask.*
exit : 1

Reopening a module restores its interface and operation names
module A { effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } sig first : [Reader] (() -> Int) fun first() { do Ask } } module B { effect Reader { Ask : () => Int; } } open A; open B; var second = fun() { do Ask } : [Reader] (() -> Int); open A; handle(handle(first() + second()) { case <Ask => k> -> k(40) }) { case <B.Ask => k> -> k(2) }
stdout : 42 : Int

